  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday there will be an upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow skies to be clear \nwith light winds. As a result at some lower-elevation locations \nthere is the potential for patchy fog to form. See the Forecast \nConfidence and Scenarios section for details. Today it will be dry \nwith well-below normal temperatures across North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. On Monday the upper-level ridge remains \nover North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. By Monday \nevening an upper-level trough begins to move over the area. This \nwill bring slightly warmer and dry weather to North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana through the day on Monday. Monday \nevening snow-levels will begin to fall and snow will begin across \nthe mountains of Southwestern Montana.\n\nOn Tuesday the upper-level trough remains over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring widespread \nprecipitation primarily in the form of lower-elevation rain and \nMountain snow. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios section for \ndetails. Due to lingering southerly flow temperatures will warm up a \nfew degrees on Tuesday but remain below seasonal averages. On \nWednesday an upper-level ridge briefly builds in over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow warm temperatures \nto persist with mostly dry weather. Thursday through Saturday an \nupper-level trough returns to North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will bring multiple rounds of \nprecipitation to the area with cooler temperatures. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFor fog this morning there is around a 30 - 50% chance along the \nRocky Mountain Front, the lower-elevations of Cascade County, and \nthe West Yellowstone area. However based on current satellite \nimagery and model soundings this is most likely due to low clouds \nand not fog. In the Helena and Gallatin Valley there is a low chance \nfor fog to form this morning as well, which is suggested in some \nmodel soundings. Based on current satellite imagery the Gallatin \nValley and West Yellowstone area have the highest chance for fog \nto form out of any of the areas listed above. \n\nFor the Monday evening through Tuesday morning system there is a 50 -\n80% chance for 4 inches of snow or greater across the higher peaks \nof the Madison and Gallatin Mountains. For the Monday evening \nthrough Tuesday morning system there is a 40 - 70% chance for 2 \ninches of snow at pass-level in the Madison and Gallatin Mountains. \nFrom Monday evening through Tuesday there is a 40 - 80% chance for a \nquarter inch of liquid precipitation or greater across Southwestern \nMontana. From Monday evening through Tuesday morning across the \nGallatin and Madison River Valleys and the Lewistown area there is \ngreater than a 60% chance for a tenth of an inch of snow or greater. \nOn Tuesday across Blaine and Fergus Counties there is a 40 - 60% \nchance for a quarter inch of total liquid precipitation or greater. \n-IG",
